course: religion-ethics
lesson: 25
title: Weddings, funerals and the people of the church
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At a church wedding a couple make vows before God and witnesses to love and be faithful «for better, for worse ... till death us do part», and exchange rings, circles with no end, as a sign of lasting love.
explain: A Christian funeral gives thanks for a person's life, comforts those who mourn and commits the person to God; at the grave the words «ashes to ashes, dust to dust» remind people that bodies return to the earth, said with the hope of resurrection.
explain: Churches have many kinds of leaders: a vicar or rector in the Church of England, a priest in Catholic, Orthodox and Anglican churches, a minister in Methodist and Presbyterian churches, a pastor in Baptist and Pentecostal churches; bishops look after many churches, and the Pope, the Bishop of Rome, leads the Catholic Church.
explain: Many Protestant and Anglican churches have women ministers and bishops, while Catholic and Orthodox churches ordain only men as priests; Christians read the Bible differently here and each side gives reasons.
explain: Deacons, elders, church wardens and many volunteers care for people, buildings and money, and monks and nuns give their whole lives to prayer and service.
explain: For a child today this means the church is not just a building or one leader on Sunday, but a whole family of people serving, and that you can help too.
fact: At a church wedding the couple make vows before God and witnesses, «for better, for worse ... till death us do part».
fact: Christians see marriage as a lifelong promise.
fact: Churches differ on same-sex marriage and on remarriage after divorce; these are disputed questions.
fact: Wedding rings are circles with no end, a sign of lasting love.
fact: A Christian funeral gives thanks for a life, comforts those who mourn and commits the person to God.
fact: Christians grieve at a death, but with the hope of resurrection.
fact: The words «ashes to ashes, dust to dust» are said at a burial or cremation.
fact: A vicar or rector leads a Church of England parish; priests serve in Catholic, Orthodox and Anglican churches.
fact: Methodist and Presbyterian churches usually have ministers; Baptist and Pentecostal churches often have pastors.
fact: Bishops are senior leaders who look after many churches in an area called a diocese.
fact: The Pope is the Bishop of Rome and leader of the Catholic Church worldwide.
fact: Catholics see the Pope as the successor of the apostle Peter; other churches do not accept his authority.
fact: Many Protestant and Anglican churches have women ministers and bishops; Catholic and Orthodox churches ordain (make into priests) only men; this is disputed.
fact: One side points out that Jesus chose twelve men as his apostles; the other side stresses Paul's words that all are one in Christ, male and female.
fact: Monks and nuns live in communities devoted to prayer and service, with vows of poverty (owning nothing of their own), chastity (not marrying) and obedience (doing as their leaders say).
exercise: What do a couple promise at a church wedding? | To love and be faithful for better, for worse | To share their money with the church for ever | To go to church every Sunday without fail | To live in the same town as their parents
exercise: Why are wedding vows made in front of witnesses? | So the promise is public and others can support it | So the guests know when to start the party | Because the law forbids weddings that have no music or singing | So the vicar can check the rings are real gold
exercise: Why is a ring a good sign of marriage? | A circle has no end, like lasting love | Gold is the only metal allowed in church | It shows how rich the couple's families are | It keeps the hand warm during the service
exercise: How do Christians see marriage? | As a lifelong promise | As a contract for ten years | As a party with no promises | As a rule only for priests
exercise: Do all churches agree about remarriage after divorce? | No, churches differ; it is disputed | Yes, all churches forbid it completely | Yes, all churches allow it without question | No, only the Pope is allowed to remarry
exercise: What is a Christian funeral for? | Thanking God for a life and comforting mourners | Deciding who inherits the person's house | Telling everyone the person's mistakes | Making sure the person is not forgotten by the bank
exercise: What does «ashes to ashes, dust to dust» remind people? | Bodies return to the earth, but there is hope | Everyone must be buried in a sandy place by the sea | Fires are dangerous and must always be put out quickly | The church needs sweeping out after every funeral
exercise: Why do Christians say they grieve «with hope»? | They believe in resurrection and life with God | They hope the funeral will be short | They hope to inherit something valuable from the person who died | They believe sadness is a sin
exercise: What is the leader of a Church of England parish usually called? | A vicar or rector | A cardinal or abbot | An imam | A rabbi
exercise: What are leaders usually called in Methodist and Presbyterian churches? | Ministers | Bishops | Cardinals | Abbots
exercise: What does a bishop do? | Looks after many churches in a diocese | Leads the singing in one small church with the choir | Rings the church bells on Sundays | Guards the church doors at night
exercise: Who is the Pope? | The Bishop of Rome, leader of the Catholic Church | The leader of every Christian church on earth | The oldest vicar in each country | The king of Italy, chosen by priests
exercise: Why do Catholics link the Pope to the apostle Peter? | They see the Pope as Peter's successor | The Pope's first name is always Peter | Peter built the Pope's house in Rome | Peter wrote the rules for choosing Popes
exercise: Do other churches accept the Pope's authority? | No, Protestant and Orthodox churches do not | Yes, every church obeys the Pope | Yes, but only at Christmas and Easter | No, and Catholics no longer follow him either
exercise: Which churches ordain only men as priests? | Catholic and Orthodox churches | All Methodist and Baptist churches | Every church in the world | No church does that today
exercise: What do deacons, elders and church wardens do? | Care for people, buildings and money | Write new books of the Bible each year | Choose the next Pope in a secret meeting | Judge who may enter heaven and who may not
exercise: What vows do monks and nuns take? | Poverty, chastity and obedience | Wealth, fame and power | Silence, fasting and travel | Marriage, children and work
exercise: What is a nun? | A woman who lives in a community devoted to prayer and service | A woman who leads the choir in a parish church | A girl who has just been confirmed by a bishop | A woman who works in a church cafe on Sundays, serving tea and cake
test: «my aunt's wedding, they said till death do us part, so they can't ever argue?» asks a little cousin. Best answer? | They promise to stay faithful in good and bad times, arguments too | Yes, married people are never allowed to argue | No, the promise only lasts a few years | The words are only old poetry, they mean nothing
test: Your classmate Noor, who is Muslim, asks: «Why did they say ashes to ashes? Is that a curse?» Best answer? | It means bodies return to the earth, said with hope | Yes, it is a curse on the person who died | It means the body is turned to gold before it is buried | It is a joke the vicar tells at funerals
test: A friend's grandma died. At break someone says «get over it, she was old». What is the kind, wise response? | Stay with your friend, listen and let them be sad | Agree with them, because old people dying is not a big deal | Tell your friend crying is babyish | Change the subject and ignore them
test: «The Pope's the boss of all Christians,» says your brother. Put him right. | He leads Catholics; other churches do not accept his authority | Yes, every Christian must obey him | No, the Pope leads the Orthodox only | The Pope is just a very famous vicar in London who is on the telly
test: Mia's church has a woman vicar. A boy says «that's not allowed, girls can't be priests». What is the fair answer? | Churches disagree; many allow it, Catholic and Orthodox do not | He's right, no church allows women leaders | Women have always led every church | It is a law of the country, not a church question
test: «Bishop? that's a chess piece innit» laughs a kid. What is a bishop in the church? | A senior leader caring for many churches in a diocese | A priest whose only job is to play chess with the Pope | A helper who hands out the hymn books at the church door | The person who climbs the tower to ring the church bells
test: An olympiad judge asks: «What reasons might each side give on women as priests?» Best-reasoned answer? | One side follows Jesus' choice of male apostles; the other stresses that all are equal in Christ | One side is clever and the other side is stupid | Nobody gives any reasons, it is just habit | Both sides agree; there is no real debate
test: «Why'd anyone become a nun, sounds boring,» says your sister. What is a fair answer? | Some people want to give their whole life to prayer and service | Nuns are forced to join when they are babies | People become nuns so that they can avoid going to school as children | Nuns get paid very well by the Pope
test: Your mate says, «Church is just the vicar on Sunday, the rest is nothing.» What's a better view? | Many people serve: wardens, elders, deacons, volunteers | He is right, only the vicar does anything | The bishop does all of the work in every single church himself | Churches run themselves without any people
test: A judge follows up: «What could a child do to help their church?» | Help with tidying, reading, welcoming or caring for others | Nothing, children are not allowed to help | Only put money in the collection plate, and nothing else at all | Tell adults what they are doing wrong
test: «priest, vicar, pastor, same thing lol» says a friend. Which answer is most accurate? | All lead churches, but names differ by church | They are three ranks of leader, and the pastor is the highest one | Only Catholics have any church leaders | A pastor is a priest who looks after sheep
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Christian understanding of marriage vows? | A public and lifelong undertaking of fidelity made before God and witnesses | A private arrangement of fixed duration terminable at will | A financial contract concluded between two families | An undertaking binding solely upon members of the clergy
exam: Identify the office held by the Pope. | Bishop of Rome and head of the Catholic Church | Head of all Christian denominations without exception | Senior minister of the Methodist Church | Archbishop of a diocese within the Church of England
exam: Ascertain the signification of the words «ashes to ashes, dust to dust» as pronounced at a Christian committal. | That the body returns to the earth, in hope of resurrection | That the deceased is hereby condemned to perdition for his sins | That the remains are to be scattered at sea | That the funeral rites are hereby concluded
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the ordination of women. | Many Protestant and Anglican churches ordain women; the Catholic and Orthodox churches do not | All Christian churches ordain women to every office | No Christian church at present ordains women | Only the Catholic Church ordains women as priests
